{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":83043,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"836a98fa-a77b-465d-aa46-d875dfbf302a","total_cost_usd":0.8392915000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63404,"cache_read_input_tokens":20655,"output_tokens":5916,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":63404,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5916,"cache_read_input_tokens":20655,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":63404},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":46904,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.047014,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":5916,"cacheReadInputTokens":20655,"cacheCreationInputTokens":63404,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.7922775000000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"The config flies — every band is reachable and every hold lands soft — but the simulator contradicts the params on the two things the ablation model exists to produce: skimming is measured to cool the entry far less than the params assume, and a direct plunge is the cheapest descent from every band and load, so the 2-4 pass optimum in §2.3.1 does not exist as flown.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"cheapest_descent_is_always_a_single_plunge\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"params ablation.optimal_skims asserts the cheapest descent is 0 skims from suborbital, 1 from low and 2 from high, and §2.3.1 requires the cheapest path be 2-4 committed passes, 'never a single plunge and never feathering'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"cheapest_pass_count is 1 in all nine verification descents. Plate cost rises monotonically with pass count in every case: suborbital full hold 23.5 / 28.0 / 31.0 / 33.8% for 1/2/3/4 passes; low full hold 21.2 / 28.0 / 31.3 / 34.2%; high full hold 19.3 / 29.4 / 33.3 / 36.2%. The second pass costs 4.5-10.1 points of plate and saves nothing, so the U-curve has no interior minimum at any band or load.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_cooling_is_a_third_of_what_params_assume\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier is [1, 0.84, 0.72, 0.68] — one skim assumed to remove 16% of committed-entry peak heat and three to remove 32%, and every entry in the params' cost_curve is computed from those factors.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured at the same entry depth the params assume (entry_depth_m 0): suborbital [1, 0.898, 0.730, 0.730], low [1, 0.956, 0.912, 0.867], high [1, 0.971, 0.944, 0.918]. In the low band one skim buys 4.4% instead of 16%, and in the high band 2.9% — the band the params lean on hardest is the one where skimming does least. Only suborbital comes near the assumed curve, and it saturates hard after two skims (0.730 then 0.730).\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_benefit_inverts_across_bands\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"§2.3.7 and the params' optimal_skims both have the high band needing the most aerobraking — 2 skims where suborbital needs 0 — because 'each altitude band up is disproportionately hotter to return from'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Skim effectiveness runs the wrong way: the three-skim multiplier is 0.730 (suborbital), 0.867 (low), 0.918 (high). The band that most needs cooling gets the least from the technique the design assigns it. Entry depth dominates instead — moving the committed entry from 0 m to 39000 m deeper drops high-band peak heat from 157.1 to 100.6 (36%), against the 8.2% three skims buy at the same depth. The lever the design names is not the lever the simulator responds to.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"heat_index_understates_measured_peak_heat\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.heat_index of 76 / 100 / 146 (suborbital / low / high), derived in balance_notes from entry speed cubed times sqrt(beta), with reentry.heat_capacity 100 as the base plate; balance_notes concludes a direct suborbital entry 'reads 76' and is therefore safe at base gear.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The simulator's own normalisation puts an empty single-pass suborbital entry at 100, and measures direct entries at 100.0 / 126.6 / 157.1 for the three bands. The ratios (1.00 / 1.27 / 1.57) are close to the params' (1.00 / 1.32 / 1.92 relative to suborbital) for low but the high band is 18% cooler than claimed; more importantly the absolute placement is wrong — a direct low-band entry measures 126.6 against a base heat_capacity of 100, so it is over the cargo-damage line, not exactly on it. The params' claim that a direct suborbital entry is comfortably safe at 76 is an artefact of normalising to the low band rather than to a flown entry.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"cost_curve_overstates_plate_cost\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.cost_curve gives plate cost per descent as suborbital [14.03, 14.60, 18.71, 27.40], low [24.68, 21.62, 23.56, 31.63], high [57.37, 43.12, 38.41, 44.58], and balance_notes prices the lazy-run break-even off the 14.03% suborbital figure.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Flown empty, the same descents cost 12.1 / 15.6 / 18.3 / 21.0% (suborbital), 11.0 / 15.5 / 18.4 / 21.2% (low) and 10.2 / 16.1 / 19.2 / 22.0% (high). The direct high-band entry is 10.2% measured against 57.37% claimed — 5.6x — and the claimed curve's ordering (high costs 4x suborbital) does not appear at all: measured direct-entry cost is essentially flat across bands and slightly *decreasing* with altitude. The economy note that replating a suborbital run costs 42.09 against 300 of revenue is priced off a number the simulator does not reproduce.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_shallow_pass_dies_suborbital_survives_low\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"§2.3.1 makes pre-stage shallow braking the intended coarse-braking phase — 'shallow passes with the naked hull soaking slow heat, while thrust remains' — and states shallow unstaged passes are survivable because cargo damage only begins at 100% heat.\",\n      \"measured\": \"unstaged_braking measures a suborbital shallow pass at 407 peak heat, survivable false, against a low-band pass at 93, survivable true. The band the player meets first is the one where the intended technique kills them, and it is inverted relative to the design's expectation that lower is gentler. The grid agrees this is structural, not a bad number: unstaged_pass_survivable is satisfied by only 13.3% of the 324 worlds — the least achievable of the eight targets — and every one of the nine top-scoring configs fails it, with unstaged peak heat 352-462.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ballistic_coefficient_is_the_binding_quantity\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"§4.5 risk 1 predicts the U-shaped ablation curve may not tune; the charter names ballistic coefficient as the usual binding constraint on whether aerobraking exists.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The crew's config flies at staged beta 228.6 kg/m2 — well above the ~50 floor, so skimming exists but each skim removes little energy, which is exactly the measured 0.956/0.971 multipliers. In the grid, skimming_cools_the_entry is satisfied by 46.3% of worlds and every one of them sits at low beta: the top configs measure 33.3, 11.1, 44.4 and 111.1 kg/m2, and the single 444.4 kg/m2 entry in the top list is the one that fails skimming_cools_the_entry outright (multipliers 1 / 0.980 / 0.962 / 0.945 — the same shape as the crew's high band). Beta is the controlling quantity and the crew is an order of magnitude above the range where skims bite.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_lands_soft_everywhere_with_margin\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.3 under a 5 m/s soft line, and §2.3.6 makes Storage and Parachute a paired purchase because a greedy haul should drift toward the hard-landing line.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured touchdown is 3.07 empty, 3.67 half hold, 4.18 full hold — identical in all three bands, and 0.82 m/s under the soft line at full load. The params predicted 4.3 from a 1149.9 kg hold; the simulator's catalog gives 1025.3 kg (170.89 kg/slot) for a mass ratio of 1.85x rather than 1.958x. The rule holds but the tension does not: no legal load in the shipping slice comes near a hard landing, so the Storage/Parachute pairing has nothing to counterweight at base gear. Grid context: full_hold_lands_soft is satisfied by only 22.2% of worlds, so the margin is a real achievement, not an accident.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"high_band_ascent_margin_is_thin\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"balance_notes states fuel_tank t2 (2400 kg) reaches the high band with 609 kg spare, gating bands behind tank tiers.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The verification ascents fly the base 1400 kg tank and reach all three bands: suborbital 135.8 kg remaining (9.7%), low 83.91 kg (6.0%), high 44.55 kg (3.2%). So the tank tiers do not gate the bands at all — the base ship reaches the high band — and the 3.2% high-band margin leaves roughly 105 m/s at loaded mass against a ~42 m/s deorbit burn, which is enough to get home but not enough for a mistake. Grid context: fuel_margin_sane is satisfied by only 34.9% of worlds, the second-least achievable target.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.84, 0.72, 0.68],\n      \"proposed\": [1, 0.94, 0.89, 0.87],\n      \"reason\": \"Replaces an assumed cooling curve with the measured mid-band one (low band [1, 0.956, 0.912, 0.867]) so every cost_curve figure downstream is computed from something the simulator reproduces; it costs the params their argument that skimming is worth the plate, which is the point of finding 2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.heat_cost_exponent\",\n      \"current\": 2.4,\n      \"proposed\": 3.2,\n      \"reason\": \"With honest skim multipliers the only way an interior minimum can exist is a steeper heat term, so a hot committed entry is punished hard enough to outweigh the fatigue toll of an extra cycle; it costs high-band survivability at base gear and pushes heat_shield t1 from convenient to mandatory.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_growth\",\n      \"current\": 1.5,\n      \"proposed\": 1.25,\n      \"reason\": \"The measured plate curve is monotonic partly because each extra cycle costs 50% more than the last; flattening the growth lets a second and third pass be affordable enough for the steeper heat term to win, without removing the anti-feathering pressure that the toll exists to create.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.heat_index\",\n      \"current\": {\"suborbital\": 76, \"low\": 100, \"high\": 146},\n      \"proposed\": {\"suborbital\": 100, \"low\": 127, \"high\": 157},\n      \"reason\": \"Re-anchors the index to the simulator's own normalisation — measured direct entries of 100.0 / 126.6 / 157.1 — instead of to a derivation that placed suborbital at 76; it costs the balance note its claim that base gear comfortably survives a direct low-band entry, which was never true as flown.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"reentry.heat_capacity\",\n      \"current\": 100,\n      \"proposed\": 135,\n      \"reason\": \"With the index re-anchored, a base plate of 100 puts a direct low-band entry over the cargo-damage line on the band the shipping slice teaches; 135 keeps suborbital and low safe when flown well and leaves the high band's 157 genuinely unflyable without the shield upgrades, preserving 'upgrades buy margin, not immunity'.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": 3,\n      \"proposed\": 1.6,\n      \"reason\": \"A suborbital unstaged shallow pass measures 407 peak heat and does not survive, which makes the pre-stage braking phase §2.3.1 depends on unavailable in the first band; halving the multiplier brings that pass to roughly 217 — still punishing, still a reason to stage — but the grid says only 13.3% of worlds satisfy this target at all, so treat this as buying room rather than fixing the rule, and take §4.5 risk 1's named fallback (flat per-pass ablation with a hard pass cap) if it will not close.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\n      \"current\": 1400,\n      \"proposed\": 1150,\n      \"reason\": \"The base tank reaches all three bands with 3.2% left at high, so the tank tiers gate nothing; trimming base capacity restores the band gating balance_notes claims and makes fuel_tank t1 a real purchase, at the cost of a tighter suborbital first run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": 550,\n      \"proposed\": 470,\n      \"reason\": \"Measured full-hold touchdown is 4.18 m/s, not the 4.30 the params predicted, because the catalog hold is 1025.3 kg rather than 1149.9; shrinking the canopy puts a full base hold back near 4.5 m/s so the Storage upgrade has something to push over the line and the Parachute upgrade something to pull back.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The simulator is a point mass with a perfect pilot: no attitude error, so off_retrograde_penalty is never exercised and every measured peak heat is a best case. Real play will run hotter than every figure above, which argues for the heat_capacity increase and against trusting the thin high-band margins.\",\n    \"The measured skim multipliers are reported at entry_depth_m 0 and the params' curve is presumably meant for the same, but the by_entry_depth tables show the multipliers move with committed-entry depth (suborbital one-skim runs 0.898 at depth 0 and 0.976 at 39000 m). Finding 2's numbers are the strongest case for skimming in the table, not the average one — the discrepancy with the params is if anything understated.\",\n    \"I cannot separate 'skimming removes little energy' from 'the simulator's skim model is thin' with the data I have. The exploration grid gives circumstantial support — skim effectiveness tracks ballistic coefficient across 324 worlds, with the 444.4 kg/m2 config showing the same flat 0.98/0.96/0.94 shape as the crew's high band — but if the simulator's skim pass integrates too short an atmospheric path, every finding about skim value inherits that error. This is the single assumption most worth an independent check before flying the proposal.\",\n    \"The 2-4 pass optimum is reported as a design finding rather than a tuning one on the strength of the ablation model's structure, not on a grid sweep of the ablation constants — the exploration grid varies world and ship parameters, not cycle_toll_growth or heat_cost_exponent. My three ablation proposals are therefore untested moves in a space nobody has swept; they should be swept before they are flown, and §4.5's flat-cost fallback remains live.\",\n    \"The verification descents report touchdown speeds identical across all three bands (3.07 / 3.67 / 4.18), which is what a terminal-velocity model gives but is suspicious if the simulator is meant to integrate the descent — a heavier entry from a higher band should arrive under canopy with some residual difference. If touchdown is being computed analytically rather than flown, finding 8's margin is a property of the formula, not of the flight.\",\n    \"The exploration grid's radius values (800 m to 320 km) include entries that cannot host the design at all, so the 0.133 and 0.222 satisfaction rates are diluted by worlds nobody would ship. They still rank the targets correctly against each other, which is the use I put them to, but the absolute rates should not be read as 'only 13% of reasonable worlds'.\",\n    \"Nothing in the sweep exercises EVA, tethers, shear, tow fees or the compactor, so §2.3.4's magnet claims and §2.3.3's fee clamp are untested here; the economy-balancer's arithmetic on those is unchallenged by me, which is not the same as confirmed.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":81074,"ttft_stream_ms":1611,"time_to_request_ms":219,"type":"result","duration_ms":81090,"uuid":"550b23c4-b829-4bdb-aa7a-d13a1f2cc62f"}
